    caseText: `
    <p>Helix Medical Devices Ltd. is developing a portable patient-monitoring device intended for
    hospitals and emergency-care centres. The market already contains several competing devices.
    Customers have indicated that accuracy, battery life, portability and reliability are essential,
    while certain cosmetic features have relatively little influence on purchasing decisions.</p>
    <p>After extensive market research, the company estimates that the device can be sold for
    <strong>₹75,000</strong> per unit. The board requires a profit of <strong>22% of selling price</strong>.
    Management therefore instructed the design team to work within the resulting target cost rather than
    designing the product first and adding a profit margin later.</p>
    <p>The preliminary cost model prepared by the cross-functional team is as follows: electronic
    components ₹24,000, casing and mechanical parts ₹8,000, direct labour ₹10,000, software and testing
    ₹6,500, packaging ₹2,000, distribution and installation ₹4,000 and expected warranty/service
    cost ₹5,000 per unit.</p>
    <p>The design team initially believed the product was already inexpensive enough because the
    preliminary cost appeared competitive with current production costs. However, the management
    accountant reminded them that target costing is market-driven and that the target cost represents
    the maximum allowable cost consistent with the desired profit.</p>
    <p>During value engineering, a supplier offered a redesigned electronic module. The existing module
    costs ₹8,000 as part of the electronic component package, while the redesigned version would cost
    ₹6,400. However, it requires an additional reliability-testing cost of ₹300 per unit.</p>
    <p>A second supplier proposed a standardised casing that would reduce casing cost by ₹1,200 but would
    increase packaging cost by ₹200 because additional protective material would be required.</p>
    <p>The engineering team also found that a cosmetic display feature cost ₹900 per unit but customers
    considered it unimportant. Removing the feature would not affect medical functionality, safety or
    reliability. Management asked whether the proposed changes collectively provide sufficient cost
    reduction to achieve the target.</p>
    `,
