    caseText: `
    <p>Nova Mobility Ltd. is deciding between two designs for a compact electric delivery vehicle.
    The marketing department expects both designs to satisfy the basic customer requirement, but their
    cost structures differ significantly. Management initially favoured Design A because its development
    cost is lower and the design is simpler to manufacture.</p>
    <p>Design A requires ₹15 crore of research, development and testing expenditure. It is expected to
    sell 6,000 vehicles over its commercial life. Manufacturing cost is ₹5.80 lakh per vehicle,
    distribution and selling cost is ₹90,000 per vehicle, warranty cost is ₹40,000 per vehicle and
    end-of-life recycling cost is ₹20,000 per vehicle.</p>
    <p>Design B requires ₹24 crore of development expenditure. However, improvements in battery design
    and modular manufacturing reduce manufacturing cost to ₹5.10 lakh per vehicle. Distribution and
    selling cost is ₹95,000 per vehicle, warranty cost is ₹25,000 per vehicle and end-of-life recycling
    cost is only ₹10,000 per vehicle.</p>
    <p>The expected average selling price is ₹8.20 lakh for Design A and ₹8.35 lakh for Design B.
    Market research suggests that both products can achieve the expected sales volume if launched
    correctly.</p>
    <p>The finance director points out that comparing only the ₹15 crore and ₹24 crore development
    expenditures could be misleading. Design B's higher development expenditure is incurred before
    production but may create savings throughout manufacturing, warranty and disposal.</p>
    <p>The board asks the management accountant to determine the total life-cycle profit for each design
    and explain why the design with the higher initial investment may still be preferable.</p>
    `,
