    caseText: `
    <p>Meridian Engineering Ltd. manufactures precision components for industrial machinery. Over the
    last three years, the company has experienced increasing pressure on margins because competitors
    have entered the market with lower prices. The board therefore instructed the finance department
    to establish a stronger system for managing manufacturing costs.</p>
    <p>The company introduced annual cost budgets for each production department. Each department was
    assigned a cost target and a manager was made responsible for monitoring actual expenditure against
    the approved budget. Monthly reports showed material, labour and overhead variances. Whenever actual
    expenditure exceeded the budget, the responsible manager was required to investigate the reason and
    submit corrective action.</p>
    <p>Initially, management was pleased with the results. Several departments reduced overtime,
    controlled material usage and delayed certain discretionary expenditures. However, the production
    director noticed that most of these savings disappeared when production volumes changed. Some
    managers were also reluctant to challenge existing production methods because the existing standards
    were treated as the benchmark against which performance was measured.</p>
    <p>The chief executive then appointed a separate cost-reduction team. Unlike the departmental
    budgetary-control exercise, this team was asked to question whether existing methods, product
    specifications, materials, layouts and processes were themselves unnecessarily expensive.</p>
    <p>The team discovered that two different products used separate versions of an almost identical
    component. By standardising the component, the company could reduce purchasing complexity and
    inventory requirements. It also found that a particular production inspection was performed twice,
    even though the second inspection added little customer value.</p>
    <p>The chief executive emphasised that any proposed cost reduction must be real and permanent and
    must not impair the utility, quality or characteristics required by customers. The team was also
    instructed to monitor whether the savings continued after implementation.</p>
    `,
