    caseText: `
    <p>GreenCore Chemicals Ltd. manufactures industrial solvents. The company has traditionally treated
    environmental expenditure as a regulatory obligation rather than as a strategic management issue.
    Environmental expenses were recorded in several different accounts, including factory overhead,
    maintenance, laboratory expenditure and legal expenses.</p>
    <p>Following an increase in customer concern about sustainability, the board asked the management
    accountant to establish a more systematic environmental-cost framework. The review revealed that
    the company spent money on pollution-control equipment, environmental research, employee protective
    equipment and site feasibility studies before production began.</p>
    <p>During production, the company incurred costs for monitoring emissions, testing wastewater,
    inspecting environmental controls and preparing regulatory reports. These activities were performed
    even though no environmental incident had occurred.</p>
    <p>The review also identified costs associated with waste generated inside the factory. Some waste
    could be recycled, while other material required special handling and disposal. These costs arose
    because the production process was not completely efficient.</p>
    <p>A more serious problem occurred when a storage tank leaked. The company had to clean contaminated
    soil within its premises. Several months later, a separate incident resulted in contamination
    outside the company's property. Management was forced to pay for land restoration and faced the
    possibility of reputational damage and legal claims from affected parties.</p>
    <p>The CFO asked whether all these expenditures should simply be combined under one environmental
    cost heading. The environmental manager argued that such aggregation would hide important information
    because different types of environmental costs arise from different management failures and require
    different preventive responses.</p>
    <p>The management accountant therefore proposed classifying environmental costs according to
    prevention, appraisal, internal failure and external failure, while also considering broader
    environmental-cost classifications and physical material flows.</p>
    `,
